          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which conditions covered here have a three-character code that takes no further character?",
          "answer": "Only one condition in the retrieved material is confirmed as complete at three characters: **essential (primary) hypertension, I10**. The coding summary on hypertension says I10 \"takes none\" of the further characters that most categories need, so it can be reported as written (source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*).\n\nThe other conditions do not qualify, or the passages do not say:\n\n- **Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (J44)** needs a fourth character to record whether a complication is present (source: *Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44*).\n- **Type 2 diabetes (E11)** is described as having a three-character category, but the passage does not say whether that category is billable without further characters. I can't confirm it either way from this material.\n- **I16** appeared in the lookup results, but the passages only describe the I10 to I16 block. They do not say whether I16 is complete at three characters.\n\nThe general rule is in *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*. A three-character code is billable only if its category requires no further characters. Most categories need a fourth, fifth or sixth character. Some also need a seventh character, which records the stage of care, such as initial or subsequent treatment. When a seventh character applies but the characters before it are empty, the placeholder X fills those positions. Coders should check the full Tabular List, not just the Alphabetic Index, because the Tabular List holds the instructional notes.\n\nFor a specific claim, a trained medical coder should confirm the code against the current official ICD-10-CM tables.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
